

Q1) Why does the fuzzer AFL ship with its own custom C compiler? (In fact it ships with multiple
different custom C compilers.)

    Because the compiler has to instrument the code such that it can create new program states. AFL works on branch coverage and recording new state transitions as a proxy for new program coverage. The code has to be instrumented to create these states.

Q2) Consider the following code snippet:

    void sum(unsigned *flags) {
        for (unsigned i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            if (flags[i] > 1) {
                return;
            }
        }
        unsigned accum = 0;
        for (unsigned i = 0; i < 8; i++) {
            if (flags[i] == 1) {
                accum = accum + 1;
            }
        }
