    }

Suppose that whenever sum is invoked, the flags argument points to an array of length 8.

    a) How many possible inputs are there to sum, assuming 32-bit unsigned integers?

    ((2^32)^8) = 2^256

    b) For every single element of the 8 element array provided, there is a 1/256 chance that the input is 1. Therefore (1/256)^8 chance that the assertion will fail. 

    c) There is a higher chance, although impossible to quantify without knowledge of the internal workings of the fuzzer, that the coverage guided mutation approach will lead to finding the assertion failure faster. AFL works with random mutations of the code that is coverage guided, so it should hopefully be not long before the fuzzer realises that bit flips around 0 and 1 lead to exploring the accum = accum + 1 line for every loop iteration. 

    d) 
    void sum(unsigned * flags) {
        if (flags[0] > 1) { return; }
        if (flags[1] > 1) { return; }
        if (flags[2] > 1) { return; }
        if (flags[3] > 1) { return; }
