Q5) In random differential testing, pioneered by tools such as Csmith, we search for compiler bugs by
generating large random programs that do not exhibit undefined behaviour, and use the programs to cross-check
multiple compilers. Result mismatches indicate compiler bugs.
Give two reasons why, for a particular set of compilers, it may be impossible to find a particular bug using
random differential testing.

It can be the case that a compiler is affected by several bugs, all of which could be found via random
differential testing in principle. However, it is often the case that in practice it is not possible to find more than
one or two of the bugs. Discuss why this might be the case.

        For random program generation, the probability of finding a particular bug is difficult to near impossible as there is no way of directing generation of the code converge on a specific portion of the compiler code.


    Well, cynically, if multiple compilers are incorrect in their implementation, the bug will persist in all of outputs and the "differential" comparison will fail!

    Most importantly, since CSmith guarentees generation of code without UB, it decreases the likelihood of generating programs which could actually crash the compiler. Things like divisions or memory access are wrapped with error detection code like div 0 or unbounded access for example, and will not allow the compiler to work on dangerous code. This reduces the scope of miscompilation. 

    Compiler code is generally well documented, tested and written. Within the types of possible bugs, there are more subtle and easy to detect bugs. There is the issue that the more subtle bug is also triggered and masked when we trigger an easier to catch bug. This subtle bug remains hidden as the result of the differential testing between compilers.  




